WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.713
<v Ishita>Sixty seconds, sixty minutes... but why sixty?

00:00:06.613 --> 00:00:10.167
<v Ishita>Why not a hundred, like a hundred paise in a rupee?

00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:13.844
<v Mishu>Ten is easy for counting. But sixty is a sharing number.

00:00:13.744 --> 00:00:19.537
<v Mishu>It splits into halves, thirds, quarters, fifths, sixths, tenths, twelfths...

00:00:19.437 --> 00:00:21.057
<v Mishu>all whole numbers!

00:00:21.057 --> 00:00:26.368
<v Rohan>So three friends can share an hour fairly... twenty minutes each! And a hundred?

00:00:26.268 --> 00:00:29.184
<v Rohan>Thirty-three, and a bit left over. Sixty wins!

00:00:29.184 --> 00:00:31.532
<v Mishu>And sixty is older than any clock.

00:00:31.432 --> 00:00:39.347
<v Mishu>Thousands of years ago, in the city of Babylon in Mesopotamia, sky-watchers counted in sixties and wrote their numbers on clay tablets.

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:45.889
<v Mishu>They used sixties to measure the sky, and much later, people used the same sixties for our minutes and seconds.

00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:48.208
<v Rohan>Measure the sky? With what?

00:00:48.208 --> 00:00:54.218
<v Mishu>With angles! A full circle has three hundred and sixty degrees: six sixties!

00:00:54.118 --> 00:01:00.743
<v Mishu>The Earth turns once in twenty-four hours, so the sun seems to move fifteen degrees every hour.

00:01:00.643 --> 00:01:06.191
<v Mishu>And a degree splits into sixty minutes, and a minute into sixty seconds!

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:08.050
<v Rohan>A clock face is a circle too!

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:14.410
<v Rohan>The minute hand goes all the way round in sixty minutes, so six degrees every minute!

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.666
<v Ishita>So every time I look at a clock, I'm counting like Babylon!
